Preliminary Experiment Findings
1.	Evidence cross-check has moved from single-source FSR assumptions to FSR+ER capable orchestration.
2.	The strongest current value is report-level evidence synthesis, not raw chunk retrieval alone.
3.	Completion review explicitly treats disposition fields as supporting, not authoritative.
4.	Recent policy update aligns retrieval horizon with recurring vs non-recurring and complete/incomplete behavior.
5.	Separation of scoping outcome and physical completion evidence is now explicit in design and runtime behavior.
Runtime and Production Gaps / Uncertainties
1.	Retrieval quality variability:
•	Vector availability and source coverage differ by environment and permissions.
1.	Evidence precision risk:
•	Completion language in reports can be ambiguous; mixed-signal reports are common.
1.	ER completeness uncertainty:
•	ER source is wired but real contribution rates vary by case set.
1.	Prompt dependency:
•	Completion-review quality still depends on LLM response robustness and parse reliability.
1.	Governance and explainability:
•	Need stable quality metrics for false-positive and false-negative completion inference.
Usage Guidance
Use E6.4 outputs as:
•	a structured evidence layer for prior completion/open/deferred reasoning
•	an input to final Step 6 applicability decisioning
Do not use E6.4 outputs as:
•	deterministic proof of completion in all cases
•	replacement for SME adjudication in mixed or conflicting evidence scenarios
